# switching_circuit.scud

## 1. Module Overview & System Context
The `switching_circuit` module acts as a critical interface between the Battery Management System (BMS) and the external Load/Charger. It performs low-side power switching (charge/discharge control) and provides integrated precision shunt current sensing. It spans the High-Voltage (HV) and Low-Voltage (LV) domains, requiring robust isolation for control signals and power supplies.

Primary interfaces include:
- HV side: Battery negative terminal (BAT-), System return (PACK-).
- LV side: Control signals from the microcontroller (CTRL_CHG/DSG, STAT_CHG/DSG).
- Power: Isolated 12V gate drive from HVPS.
- Sense: Precision current sensing via a shunt resistor (SRP/SRN) provided to the `current-sensing` module.

## 2. Architectural Deviations (Baseline vs. Target)
*Baseline Reference:* `low-side-switch-reference-circuit.png`

*Removed components/blocks:*
- Evaluation-specific test headers and connectors (J1, J2, J3) used for bench testing.
- Evaluation status LEDs (D1, D2) for CHG/DSG state.

*Added components/blocks:*
- Opto-isolated level shifters for CHG/DSG control signals to interface LV (MCU) to HV.
- Gate drive protection circuitry (TVS diodes) for transient voltage spikes.
- Passive pull-down resistors ($10k\Omega$) on FET gates to ensure safety OFF-state.
- Kelvin connections for shunt sensing to minimize measurement errors.

## 3. Components Inventory
- MOSFETs (Implemented in TSX): 8x CRSS042N10N (100V, 120A, TO-263). (Primary design target Vishay-SUM70040E).
- Shunt Resistor: 1.0mΩ Precision Shunt (2512 footprint).
- Isolated Gate Drivers: TLP250 Photocoupler (`TLP250_F_`, ±1.5A peak output current, 0.5µs propagation delay), driving the 4-MOSFET bank within the 1µs target.
- Gate Resistors: 330Ω (`R_GATE_CHG`, `R_GATE_DSG`) implemented in TSX (Note: SCUD design target 10Ω - 22Ω for fast switching; 330Ω will increase switching times).
- Gate Driver Decoupling & Current Consumption: Calculated bulk capacitance requirement of $12\,\mu\text{F}$ (using $10\,\mu\text{F}$ or $15\,\mu\text{F}$ X7R ceramic capacitor with a 10x safety factor for DC bias/temperature drift, based on $360\,\text{nC}$ total gate charge and 2% allowable voltage droop of $0.3\,\text{V}$ on a $15\,\text{V}$ rail), paired with $0.1\,\mu\text{F}$ high-frequency bypass capacitors. Module current consumption: Quiescent steady-state supply current is $22\,\text{mA}$ ($2 \times 11\,\text{mA}$ max $I_{CC}$ for the two TLP250 drivers on the 12V rail), worst-case peak gate transient current is $360\,\text{mA}$ during the $1\,\mu\text{s}$ switching transition, and control input LED current is $\sim 20\,\text{mA}$ total ($10\,\text{mA}$ per active channel from the 3.3V LV side).
- Gate Pull-downs: 5.1MΩ (`R_PD_CHG`, `R_PD_DSG`) implemented in TSX (Note: SCUD design target 10kΩ for noise immunity).
- Current Sense RC Filter: 100Ω resistors (`R_FILTER_SRP`, `R_FILTER_SRN`) and 100nF capacitors (`C_FILTER_SRP`, `C_FILTER_SRN`).

## 4. Connectivity & Signal Flow
- HV Power Path: BAT- Terminal -> [Switching Array] -> PACK- Terminal.
- Current Sensing: Shunt placed in the path, with Kelvin taps (SRP/SRN) routed through RC filter to the `current-sensing` module.
- Control Flow: MCU (LV) -> Opto-isolated Digital Control -> Gate Drivers (HV) -> MOSFET Gates.
- Status Flow: MOSFET Gate/Source Voltage Monitoring -> Opto-isolated Digital Status -> MCU (LV).
- Gate Drive Power: Isolated 12V Power Trace from `high-voltage-power-supply` to Gate Drivers.

## 5. Uncertainties, Assumptions & Confidence
- Status logic threshold levels need definition (exact $V_{DS}$ for logic high/low).
- Gate Driver Sizing: The driver IC must support a peak source/sink current of at least 360mA to charge the combined gate capacitance of 4 MOSFETs in 1 µs ($Q_{g,total} = 360 \text{nC}$).
- Gate Resistor & Pull-down Values: The initial design adopts 330Ω gate resistors and 5.1MΩ pull-down resistors directly from the baseline reference circuit. These values will be evaluated and optimized during bench testing to balance switching speed (1 µs target) and noise immunity.

Note: FET thermal performance and VDD1 power source ambiguity are considered handled by system-level design evolution.


